oh here's what happens  when you change different variables  here's what happens if you rotate it it  was a super helpful tool for a lot of us  in the class especially since we're very  visual learners and so going into that  when we started to do more projects into  it and she started her computational  thinking Club I thought it would be a  great place to learn a little bit more  about coding and maybe try and get into  it again and so I went to a few meetings  and I thought it was such an interesting  idea and a very cool language to learn  especially in comparison with my three  days doing Java  because it they had all these built-in  functions they had all this stuff that  was very not self-explanatory but very  easily comprehensible where you could  tell like based on the name of the  function what it did or if you'd had any  questions it was super simple to look it  up in the function like thing and in the  elementary introduction book and so  through that I really got into coding  and when it came to the end of the year  for our final class projects I thought  doing something with Mathematica would  be a super cool way to really explore it  and get more into it and thus my  adventure in Mathematica began my  partner and I decided to be a little  more creative and make it harder on  ourselves so instead of finding a  problem in our textbook we decided to  make ourselves a problem and we used  Mathematica to code this lovely little  Candyland we have up here and we used  all kinds of surfaces we found and all  kinds of mesh functions and color  functions to make it look really nice  and then we used our calculus knowledge  from in class to define gradients  contours and all kinds of stuff for this  little plot and through that I learned a  lot more about Mathematica and the inner  workings how syntax works that kind of  stuff  and going into that project taking on  building this Candyland with very very  little Mathematica experience was very  difficult but also very fun to see how  you could go into it knowing nothing and  learn so much just by like testing it  out going through the elementary  introduction the documentation center  and all that and through that I got even  more into it and I thought I really want  to continue on with this Mathematica  seems super fun and interesting and so  when this broad approached us with the  option to do a summer program with  augment software I thought that would be  a very fun way to continue on and to  learn more and so we got connected with  Eric we did a lot more in the elementary  introduction book and learned the we  started from the bottom the very basics  of Mathematica and the inner workings  all kinds of stuff like that and he  taught us more about computational  thinking and how to use the  documentation center and all kinds of  stuff just to figure out how to sell  a problem even if you know nothing about  the functions that you would need to  solve it and so through that when we  went into advanced topics which is our  project space Mathematica class at our  school through that all of my work with  augment carried with me and so when we  went to the beginning of the year and  we're learning the very basics of  Mathematica over again for people who  don't have any experience with it it was  very helpful to be like oh even though I  only started this a few months ago I  still am able to help others who are  brand new with this and just seeing the  the amount of growth and knowledge that  I had gained over a few months to doing  just a few projects was a very  interesting comparison with a lot of  other like languages and projects and  things that I've taken on because it was  so easy to understand and to get better  at and thus I began helping other  students in the class with their  projects when they were having some  trouble you know helping them debug find  a bracket here and there and then when  she approached us with the opportunity  to help out with a computational  thinking Club at school I thought that  would be a great opportunity to help  other students and so we're there in the  class helping other students and after  school in the club we're helping people  who have zero Mathematica experience we  sit with them we help make sure that  they can keep up with everything in the  presentation answer any questions they  have and it's just incredible to go  completely full circle in probably about  eight months from sitting in the  computational thinking Club knowing  nothing doing one of her activities to  being there helping her run the  activities helping new students with  their first introductions to Mathematica  and then creating our own notebooks and  interactive projects to do so we'll turn  it back over to Miss Brown to discuss  our future so I've got lots of ideas and  of course coming to a conference like  this always just means more ideas but  one of the things we want to do is  continue to advertise and recruit  students throughout the year it doesn't  have to be like if you miss the boat in  the first two weeks you you can't join  us we want to continue to develop new  activities we want I  want to get more student-led work so  it's less of me doing the instruction  more the students the continue doing  these computational conversations those  were some of our most fun and  interesting moments last year the  there's additional Wolfram resources I  want to explore computational thinking  initiatives resources the Wolfram  challenges Wolfram you the notebook  archive which could also be a great  place for students to publish and share  their own work with the world and  continue working with the elementary  introduction book I want to continue  developing the Future student leaders so  when these fine students graduate I'm  not starting from scratch next year  that's one of the challenges certainly  in an educational setting is you know  making sure you have that continuity so  if you are interested in seeing a little  bit more about what we're doing or  you're curious about all these notebooks  and things these images that are coming  up they are from notebooks on our  website it's Abby math comm /ct for  computational thinking the computational  thinking initiatives website has  additional resources if you're thinking  of something you might want to start up  in your own environment continue using  the text and even the some of the  articles that Stephen Wolfram has  published recently about computational  thinking on his blog have been really  kind of motivating and guiding in terms  of this idea of what is computational  thinking and what are these essays and  having the students read them and see  some of the examples that he's presented  there has really helped us kind of get  things rolling as well so I think we  have a couple minutes for some  additional questions but I do want to  thank everybody for coming in and  sharing your time with our presentation  today and we'll be around this afternoon  and tomorrow as well  so thank you very much  you
 

